    "values": [
      {
        "value": "endangered",
        "source": "elcat-v2024.1",
        "note": "At present, Lakurumau is still vital and widely used by the older generations (age ca. 40+) in their daily life, but is not always passed on to children. In a few households, Lakurumau is still the primary language, spoken on a daily basis by elderly family members as well as by children. In most families, however, and especially in mixed households, the intergenerational language is Tok Pisin, the lingua franca of Papua New Guinea. In general, children and teenagers still have a fairly good passive knowledge of Lakurumau but many of them have limited active competence and some children have almost no active competence at all. [Endangerment.Certainty: 0.8]",
        "scale": "elcat-v2024.1"
      },
